[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't realize it's the Buddha.

9
00:00:57,552 --> 00:01:00,273
[SPEAKER_00]: And so he says, all right, let me teach you the dhamma.

10
00:01:01,030 --> 00:01:07,432
[SPEAKER_00]: And there are a number of things that he goes into, namely the six elements.

11
00:01:08,672 --> 00:01:12,433
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he talks about equanimity.

12
00:01:13,313 --> 00:01:21,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, first, it's after the consciousness element, he goes into equanimity and kind of

13
00:01:23,295 --> 00:01:26,456
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it would be... basically, you have to start with what you have.

14
00:01:52,344 --> 00:01:59,947
[SPEAKER_05]: In other words, you know, like six elements, well, you're not going to be contemplating the fifth or the sixth, unless you discern the first one.

15
00:02:00,927 --> 00:02:07,430
[SPEAKER_05]: So, for somebody, at the starting point, the first element is all six elements.

16
00:02:08,130 --> 00:02:10,170
[SPEAKER_05]: You know, the first jhāna is all four jhānas.

17
00:02:12,971 --> 00:02:14,312
[SPEAKER_05]: You won't be even

18
00:02:15,257 --> 00:02:21,542
[SPEAKER_05]: Correctly being able to think about the second or the third, let alone fourth, if you have not established yourself in the first.

19
00:02:21,703 --> 00:02:22,483
[SPEAKER_05]: It's inconceivable.

20
00:02:23,104 --> 00:02:25,065
[SPEAKER_05]: Even theoretically, you will not be able to grasp it.

21
00:02:26,106 --> 00:02:29,669
[SPEAKER_05]: And same goes for any aspect of contemplation.

22
00:02:29,750 --> 00:02:39,418
[SPEAKER_05]: So, in case... in terms of six elements or casinos and so on, the first one is the direction of all the elements that you can develop.

23
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:45,442
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what is the first most obvious element that you have paired with?

24
00:02:46,362 --> 00:02:46,562
[SPEAKER_05]: Earth.

25
00:02:48,323 --> 00:02:49,424
[SPEAKER_05]: No, more obvious than that.

26
00:02:49,644 --> 00:02:50,064
[SPEAKER_05]: Let's see.

27
00:02:50,204 --> 00:02:50,404
[SPEAKER_05]: Earth.

28
00:02:50,744 --> 00:02:50,944
[SPEAKER_05]: Earth.

29
00:02:51,124 --> 00:02:51,424
[SPEAKER_05]: Okay.

30
00:02:51,585 --> 00:02:53,245
[SPEAKER_05]: So, for me, that's an idea.

31
00:02:54,026 --> 00:02:55,346
[SPEAKER_05]: For you, maybe it's more practical.

32
00:02:55,386 --> 00:02:58,568
[SPEAKER_05]: But, generally, what's something that you cannot avoid?

33
00:02:59,028 --> 00:03:01,809
[SPEAKER_05]: Whether you take it as an idea or not, you are paired with it.

34
00:03:01,889 --> 00:03:03,850
[SPEAKER_05]: So...

35
00:03:03,890 --> 00:03:04,350
[SPEAKER_05]: The body.

36
00:03:04,370 --> 00:03:04,770
[SPEAKER_05]: The body.

37
00:03:04,910 --> 00:03:08,352
[SPEAKER_05]: It's your... basically, it's the direction of the elements, of all of them.

38
00:03:10,397 --> 00:03:14,558
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's how you start to practically contemplate it.

39
00:03:15,038 --> 00:03:18,599
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, in order to start to practically contemplate it, first you need to contain the body.

40
00:03:20,339 --> 00:03:35,822
[SPEAKER_05]: Because for as long as a person is acting out of greed, aversion and delusion, eh... the body that exists for them, so to speak, the body that they would see when they look at what the body is, is the body on the level of sense objects.

41
00:03:36,342 --> 00:03:39,303
[SPEAKER_05]: Because that's where they're already dependent on.

42
00:03:40,988 --> 00:03:44,591
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's already not the body seen accurately enough.

43
00:03:45,291 --> 00:03:47,293
[SPEAKER_05]: That's not that immediate body you're paired with.

44
00:03:47,333 --> 00:03:52,236
[SPEAKER_05]: That's kind of the body that's now the result of your sensual proliferation and non-restraint.

45
00:03:54,018 --> 00:04:02,664
[SPEAKER_05]: So, if a person contains himself rightly, then that which is left there, within that containment, approximates the body more accurately.

46
00:04:03,324 --> 00:04:05,066
[SPEAKER_05]: That which the body is, in your experience.

47
00:04:07,187 --> 00:04:09,569
[SPEAKER_05]: But fundamentally, eh...

48
00:04:11,572 --> 00:04:30,270
[SPEAKER_05]: In order to then, on top of that, write containment, in order to discern that which body is in your experience rightly, you have to learn how to attend to it indirectly, as we spoke before.

49
00:04:30,991 --> 00:04:35,896
[SPEAKER_05]: Any direct attempt at attending to your body is going to be attending to the sense objects.

50
00:04:37,007 --> 00:04:40,709
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's not going to be the body on the level of sense organs.

51
00:04:42,130 --> 00:04:44,831
[SPEAKER_05]: So, then it's, yeah, learning how to see it peripherally.

52
00:04:46,012 --> 00:05:02,120
[SPEAKER_05]: In other words, whatever you're currently attending, without shifting your attention too much, you want to discern that this body has to be implied in whatever your mind is currently preoccupied with.

53
00:05:02,140 --> 00:05:05,082
[SPEAKER_05]: It has to be there as an underlying basis.

54
00:05:05,953 --> 00:05:13,877
[SPEAKER_05]: And if you keep discerning that sufficient amount of times, it will then reveal itself, so to speak.

55
00:05:14,517 --> 00:05:17,999
[SPEAKER_05]: That which you are discerning as implicit, is factually implicit.

56
00:05:18,039 --> 00:05:21,141
[SPEAKER_05]: That body is factually there, as a base of disappearance.

57
00:05:21,161 --> 00:05:28,404
[SPEAKER_05]: It's just that you, again, you overlook it, you over-attend it, or you completely ignore it.

58
00:05:29,365 --> 00:05:29,525
[SPEAKER_05]: But

59
00:05:30,534 --> 00:05:35,358
[SPEAKER_05]: Attending to it indirectly, in other words, you attend to something else, so to speak, whatever takes your attention.

60
00:05:35,518 --> 00:05:37,199
[SPEAKER_05]: It doesn't matter what you attend, basically.

61
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:48,629
[SPEAKER_05]: What matters is to recognize that whatever you're attending, the source of this experience as a whole, of attending to this flow board now, is the existing body as the sixth sense space, here and now.

62
00:05:49,369 --> 00:05:55,174
[SPEAKER_05]: That is the... that image becomes the implicit base for everything that I'm engaged with.

63
00:05:56,495 --> 00:05:58,777
[SPEAKER_05]: And because things are factually so,

64
00:06:00,303 --> 00:06:02,384
[SPEAKER_05]: Then, that image will settle in, so to speak.

65
00:06:03,484 --> 00:06:06,545
[SPEAKER_05]: Then, you wouldn't have to keep evoking it rightly.

66
00:06:07,105 --> 00:06:10,586
[SPEAKER_05]: It will be seen as... but no, it can't be otherwise.

67
00:06:10,606 --> 00:06:17,127
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, even if I forget about the body and don't have these images of the body being implicit in this experience as a whole, it is so.

68
00:06:19,028 --> 00:06:22,909
[SPEAKER_05]: And that understanding has kind of sunk deeper and sunk through, so to speak.

69
00:06:23,009 --> 00:06:24,289
[SPEAKER_05]: It's sort of sunk in.

70
00:06:25,869 --> 00:06:27,830
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's already the contemplation of the element.

71
00:06:29,097 --> 00:06:31,798
[SPEAKER_05]: The element of worth, obviously, in this case, then you can take it further.

72
00:06:31,898 --> 00:06:35,539
[SPEAKER_05]: But, even if you don't take it further, this, in and of itself, is enough.

73
00:06:36,760 --> 00:06:46,063
[SPEAKER_05]: Mindfulness of the body or non-forgetfulness of the implicit body, to the extent necessary, it's enough for the final knowledge, as the Satipaṭṭhāna Sutta says.

74
00:06:47,723 --> 00:06:48,463
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's exactly it.

75
00:06:48,483 --> 00:06:52,765
[SPEAKER_05]: See how... How do you not forget about something?

76
00:06:54,405 --> 00:06:56,046
[SPEAKER_05]: Do you constantly keep it in front of your eyes?

77
00:06:57,286 --> 00:06:58,687
[SPEAKER_05]: No, you keep it at the back of your mind.

78
00:06:59,732 --> 00:07:02,573
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's exactly why satipaṭṭhāna of the body is.

79
00:07:02,973 --> 00:07:04,833
[SPEAKER_05]: Or any, for that matter, practice of sati.

80
00:07:05,373 --> 00:07:05,733
[SPEAKER_04]: Memory.

81
00:07:07,533 --> 00:07:11,754
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you're not recollecting the body through directly observing it.

82
00:07:12,314 --> 00:07:17,495
[SPEAKER_05]: You're recollecting it as an image of your world, dependent upon the body.

83
00:07:21,216 --> 00:07:22,536
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's already a form of knowledge.

84
00:07:22,736 --> 00:07:23,537
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, write memory.

85
00:07:24,957 --> 00:07:25,857
[SPEAKER_05]: As we said that before.

86
00:07:29,165 --> 00:07:34,309
[SPEAKER_05]: So, just have a memory of your body being there regardless of what you're currently engaging with.

87
00:07:35,130 --> 00:07:36,731
[SPEAKER_05]: Past, present, future, doesn't matter.

88
00:07:37,252 --> 00:07:42,436
[SPEAKER_05]: Don't completely forget that your body has to be there.

89
00:07:42,456 --> 00:07:43,577
[SPEAKER_05]: And practice it on that level.

90
00:07:44,218 --> 00:07:46,179
[SPEAKER_05]: See, that's not really that hard thing to do.

91
00:07:46,720 --> 00:07:49,582
[SPEAKER_05]: What's hard is to contain yourself to stay on that level.

92
00:07:50,243 --> 00:07:56,568
[SPEAKER_05]: Because the tendency of craving, views, of course, and everything else will want to make you overly define

93
00:08:00,590 --> 00:08:02,051
[SPEAKER_05]: Feel it for what it is.

94
00:08:02,412 --> 00:08:04,273
[SPEAKER_05]: That's how I'll get to that body.

95
00:08:04,534 --> 00:08:07,516
[SPEAKER_05]: And the more you do it, the further away from the actual body you go.

96
00:08:09,258 --> 00:08:12,541
[SPEAKER_00]: Because in that case, you're actually overlooking it.

97
00:08:12,781 --> 00:08:12,981
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

98
00:08:13,081 --> 00:08:14,102
[SPEAKER_05]: In doing that.

99
00:08:14,142 --> 00:08:15,043
[SPEAKER_05]: You're over-tightening.

100
00:08:16,064 --> 00:08:20,288
[SPEAKER_05]: You're over-tightening your focus ring, basically.

101
00:08:21,069 --> 00:08:23,851
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's going to focus on something past the body.

102
00:08:24,832 --> 00:08:25,713
[SPEAKER_05]: It will shift.

103
00:08:28,579 --> 00:08:36,926
[SPEAKER_04]: And if somebody is recollecting the body in this sense correctly, then why is that white progress?

104
00:08:37,146 --> 00:08:39,749
[SPEAKER_04]: How has that come to uproot factors?

105
00:08:40,089 --> 00:08:40,769
[SPEAKER_04]: Why is that so?

106
00:08:48,116 --> 00:08:48,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Anybody else?

107
00:08:51,118 --> 00:08:55,362
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it is exactly through engaging, through

108
00:08:57,267 --> 00:09:01,929
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, engaging in unwholesome conduct or fueling the fetters that you overlooked above.

109
00:09:05,131 --> 00:09:13,115
[SPEAKER_05]: But no, what he's asking is, so you're not engaging in the conduct and you're recollecting it rightly, why is that resulting in uprooting of the fetters?

110
00:09:13,795 --> 00:09:18,197
[SPEAKER_05]: Why would that result in clearing away of impurities?

111
00:09:26,325 --> 00:09:28,227
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, because they're mutually exclusive.

112
00:09:29,367 --> 00:09:29,688
[SPEAKER_03]: But why?

113
00:09:30,809 --> 00:09:35,372
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, like I said, because whenever you're acting out of the fetters, you are necessarily overlooking the body.

114
00:09:36,073 --> 00:09:40,576
[SPEAKER_03]: And if you're not overlooking the body, it implies that you are not acting in those ways.

115
00:09:40,737 --> 00:09:43,058
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't see the body, right?

116
00:09:43,078 --> 00:09:45,040
[SPEAKER_05]: Falling into the circularity now.

117
00:09:45,060 --> 00:09:49,644
[SPEAKER_05]: No, the point is, yes, if you don't act out, and if you're rightly mindful, you're uprooting the fetters.

118
00:09:50,044 --> 00:09:50,665
[SPEAKER_05]: Question why?

119
00:09:51,966 --> 00:09:53,267
[SPEAKER_05]: Why is it uprooting the fetters?

120
00:09:54,280 --> 00:09:54,840
[SPEAKER_05]: What is it?

121
00:09:55,821 --> 00:10:06,545
[SPEAKER_05]: That when you are correctly recollecting the body, that environment is unsuitable for the persistence of the fetters, for the growth of the fetters.

122
00:10:08,030 --> 00:10:09,331
[SPEAKER_05]: It's actually killing them off.

123
00:10:09,932 --> 00:10:14,637
[SPEAKER_05]: They're fading away for as long as that right environment is maintained internally.

124
00:10:14,897 --> 00:10:15,557
[SPEAKER_05]: Why is that?

125
00:10:16,118 --> 00:10:19,682
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the kind of acute aspect of that?

126
00:10:19,842 --> 00:10:23,706
[SPEAKER_02]: The right order is basically established and maintained.

127
00:10:24,026 --> 00:10:24,226
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

128
00:10:24,366 --> 00:10:25,828
[SPEAKER_05]: And so, what does the right order mean?

129
00:10:27,369 --> 00:10:27,810
[SPEAKER_05]: What is it?

130
00:10:27,930 --> 00:10:28,270
[SPEAKER_05]: What is it?

131
00:10:30,110 --> 00:10:32,631
[SPEAKER_05]: Ownership.

132
00:10:34,052 --> 00:10:35,092
[SPEAKER_05]: Exactly.

133
00:10:35,632 --> 00:10:36,513
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's impossible.

134
00:10:36,573 --> 00:10:44,556
[SPEAKER_05]: That body that you see indirectly as an implicit phenomenon that endures regardless of present, past, future engagement, doesn't matter.

135
00:10:44,576 --> 00:10:47,117
[SPEAKER_05]: It doesn't matter the extent, the intensity of your engagements.

136
00:10:47,197 --> 00:10:48,538
[SPEAKER_05]: That body there, if it's seen,

137
00:10:49,535 --> 00:10:50,876
[SPEAKER_05]: It cannot be owned.

138
00:10:51,476 --> 00:10:52,136
[SPEAKER_05]: It's unownable.

139
00:10:52,856 --> 00:10:56,378
[SPEAKER_05]: It's seen as a thing that's not in your control thoroughly.

140
00:10:56,818 --> 00:11:09,644
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's why, if you sustain it sufficiently enough, it would, like, seep into all the other more acute, engaged, activated aspects of your experience, that they would then become equally unownable.

141
00:11:09,904 --> 00:11:11,465
[SPEAKER_05]: You'll be dispassionate towards them.

142
00:11:13,926 --> 00:11:18,948
[SPEAKER_05]: Now, obviously, what that extent is, well, that can only be measured in hindsight.

143
00:11:19,911 --> 00:11:21,814
[SPEAKER_05]: That's why you can't have too much of mindfulness.

144
00:11:24,017 --> 00:11:26,020
[SPEAKER_05]: You can't have too much of the right recollection.

145
00:11:26,320 --> 00:11:28,063
[SPEAKER_05]: If you dedicate your whole time

146
00:11:29,050 --> 00:11:38,518
[SPEAKER_05]: Every single day of just recollecting, every single moment of your waking time to not forgetting the endurance of the body here and now, it cannot result in too much.

147
00:11:38,578 --> 00:11:45,323
[SPEAKER_05]: It cannot result... Obviously, if you try to over-attempt to it and overly clarify it, well, that can be too much.

148
00:11:45,503 --> 00:11:47,825
[SPEAKER_05]: But that means that you're already not recollecting it rightly.

149
00:11:48,265 --> 00:11:49,366
[SPEAKER_05]: Because that's another question.

150
00:11:49,426 --> 00:11:50,487
[SPEAKER_05]: What is memory?

151
00:11:50,507 --> 00:11:51,368
[SPEAKER_05]: How do you remember things?

152
00:11:53,070 --> 00:11:53,370
[SPEAKER_04]: You mean...

153
00:11:53,670 --> 00:11:54,471
[SPEAKER_05]: Anything, anything.

154
00:11:54,531 --> 00:11:56,193
[SPEAKER_05]: Colloquially, just in the broadest sense.

155
00:11:57,133 --> 00:12:03,959
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, obviously, you know, putting aside the... Like, what's your name and stuff.

156
00:12:04,019 --> 00:12:09,383
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, no, putting aside the kind of, the whole, like, misguided scientific views of how memory is imprinted in your brain and whatnot.

157
00:12:09,403 --> 00:12:10,784
[SPEAKER_05]: Right, right.

158
00:12:11,705 --> 00:12:14,787
[SPEAKER_05]: That's not how it appears for you, from your point of view.

159
00:12:15,368 --> 00:12:17,890
[SPEAKER_05]: So, from your point of view, what is a memory?

160
00:12:19,412 --> 00:12:21,393
[SPEAKER_05]: How do you recollect things?

161
00:12:21,413 --> 00:12:21,973
[SPEAKER_05]: It's an image.

162
00:12:22,673 --> 00:12:23,414
[SPEAKER_05]: It's an image, yeah.

163
00:12:23,514 --> 00:12:24,294
[SPEAKER_05]: But how do you do that?

164
00:12:24,314 --> 00:12:25,234
[SPEAKER_05]: How do you recollect things?

165
00:12:26,095 --> 00:12:27,215
[SPEAKER_05]: By unattending.

166
00:12:28,896 --> 00:12:29,536
[SPEAKER_05]: By unattending.

167
00:12:29,876 --> 00:12:30,137
[SPEAKER_05]: Sure.

168
00:12:30,257 --> 00:12:38,180
[SPEAKER_05]: By unattending, like, exactly, by being, by becoming, by unabsorbing from whatever you might have been absorbed in.

169
00:12:39,449 --> 00:12:40,810
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you have to kind of step back.

170
00:12:42,130 --> 00:12:44,271
[SPEAKER_05]: That's already what's known as an act of reflection.

171
00:12:45,332 --> 00:12:49,594
[SPEAKER_05]: Step back on your situation, even if you don't necessarily think in terms of your situation.

172
00:12:50,595 --> 00:12:52,335
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's like, oh, remember something.

173
00:12:52,436 --> 00:12:54,637
[SPEAKER_05]: And you could even kind of, oh, remember something from yesterday.

174
00:12:54,697 --> 00:12:55,917
[SPEAKER_05]: Give me an event from yesterday.

175
00:12:56,017 --> 00:12:58,459
[SPEAKER_05]: Oh, can you remember something from approximately 10 years ago?

176
00:12:58,859 --> 00:13:01,500
[SPEAKER_05]: And you count the numbers and remember, oh, yeah, that was like 10 years ago.

177
00:13:02,741 --> 00:13:06,983
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you could selectively even remember various other things.

178
00:13:08,730 --> 00:13:22,319
[SPEAKER_04]: It's also relational, like memory not being isolated images, but it's sort of like a web, like different memories, so the more it's related, the easier it is to recollect certain things.

179
00:13:22,519 --> 00:13:24,600
[SPEAKER_05]: Yes, they remind you of other things and so on.

180
00:13:25,281 --> 00:13:28,263
[SPEAKER_05]: But, are you really doing the remembering?

181
00:13:30,184 --> 00:13:31,304
[SPEAKER_05]: Is that something that you do?

182
00:13:33,026 --> 00:13:36,568
[SPEAKER_05]: As in, do you just go and find that memory?

183
00:13:39,593 --> 00:13:39,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry?

184
00:13:40,393 --> 00:13:43,115
[SPEAKER_01]: You are... you, in inverted commas... No, no, no.

185
00:13:43,135 --> 00:13:43,855
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean, colloquially.

186
00:13:43,875 --> 00:13:44,435
[SPEAKER_05]: I'm not...

187
00:13:44,455 --> 00:13:46,596
[SPEAKER_05]: I'm not, like, trying to catch on a jargon now.

188
00:13:47,077 --> 00:13:48,777
[SPEAKER_05]: You, the... but there is no you and stuff like that.

189
00:13:48,817 --> 00:13:48,977
[SPEAKER_05]: No, no.

190
00:13:48,997 --> 00:13:49,598
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean, just you.

191
00:13:49,818 --> 00:13:51,058
[SPEAKER_05]: Do you... do you remember it?

192
00:13:51,118 --> 00:13:51,879
[SPEAKER_05]: How do you remember it?

193
00:13:54,140 --> 00:13:54,700
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, it's presented.

194
00:13:54,720 --> 00:13:55,461
[SPEAKER_05]: It's presented.

195
00:13:56,781 --> 00:13:57,842
[SPEAKER_05]: So, presented to what?

196
00:13:59,423 --> 00:13:59,863
[SPEAKER_03]: To attach.

197
00:14:01,343 --> 00:14:03,164
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, yeah, that's the way of saying it.

198
00:14:03,224 --> 00:14:07,645
[SPEAKER_05]: Basically, your mind is that which has memories.

199
00:14:07,825 --> 00:14:11,106
[SPEAKER_05]: That mind you're paired with.

200
00:14:11,126 --> 00:14:12,006
[SPEAKER_05]: They exist for it.

201
00:14:12,246 --> 00:14:13,286
[SPEAKER_05]: They're in its own domain.

202
00:14:13,667 --> 00:14:15,387
[SPEAKER_05]: They're not in your domain, so to speak.

203
00:14:16,807 --> 00:14:20,048
[SPEAKER_05]: So, everything is already implicit to a degree, like whatever now.

204
00:14:20,088 --> 00:14:23,189
[SPEAKER_05]: It has sort of the significance of being here.

205
00:14:23,469 --> 00:14:25,250
[SPEAKER_05]: It's shaped by the whole

206
00:14:26,085 --> 00:14:30,786
[SPEAKER_05]: Fresh memory of coming here, deciding to come here, for one reason or the other.

207
00:14:31,306 --> 00:14:43,089
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's not like that you're always entirely encapsulated in the present moment, without any kind of connection with these things that were before, or things that are implicit, as they're going to be after.

208
00:14:44,369 --> 00:14:49,130
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's the whole, like, past is already in your present, implicit, and so is the future.

209
00:14:50,190 --> 00:14:53,811
[SPEAKER_05]: So, past is present, future is present, and present is present.

210
00:14:54,716 --> 00:14:56,197
[SPEAKER_05]: That's already how things exist.

211
00:14:56,898 --> 00:15:05,004
[SPEAKER_05]: And from that point of view, you realize images that you have now, immediately, are already on the level of that faculty of memory.

212
00:15:06,084 --> 00:15:09,147
[SPEAKER_05]: The current images, you realize there is no difference.

213
00:15:10,221 --> 00:15:13,883
[SPEAKER_05]: The only difference is like, oh, is it like here now or like ten minutes ago?

214
00:15:14,423 --> 00:15:19,086
[SPEAKER_05]: But, from your point of view, where your mind is, there are images that present itself.

215
00:15:19,106 --> 00:15:30,612
[SPEAKER_05]: That's why, again, if that mind gets expanded, as the suttas describe, gets fully abandoned sensuality, purifies itself from all things, becomes very pliable, well, you would remember far more than ten years or ten lifetimes.

216
00:15:30,652 --> 00:15:31,953
[SPEAKER_05]: You could remember a lot more.

217
00:15:33,013 --> 00:15:38,196
[SPEAKER_05]: Because, again, it was the mind and images existing in the same sense that I exist with the I-objects.

218
00:15:39,870 --> 00:15:51,879
[SPEAKER_05]: So, the only reason a person might feel like I need to figure out the workings of it, is if they put themselves in that relationship as I am doing this and this is relevant for me, but you don't need to figure out the relationship.

219
00:15:51,939 --> 00:15:53,320
[SPEAKER_05]: How does the eye see?

220
00:15:54,041 --> 00:15:55,382
[SPEAKER_05]: How does the nose smell?

221
00:15:55,922 --> 00:15:57,163
[SPEAKER_05]: How does the mind think?

222
00:15:57,824 --> 00:15:58,404
[SPEAKER_05]: It's irrelevant.

223
00:15:59,605 --> 00:16:01,667
[SPEAKER_05]: All you need to do is develop this passion towards it.

224
00:16:03,728 --> 00:16:05,690
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's how you would understand how it works.

225
00:16:07,548 --> 00:16:13,210
[SPEAKER_05]: But by wanting to figure it out on that specific overly attending level, you are appropriating it.

226
00:16:13,370 --> 00:16:14,951
[SPEAKER_05]: You're making it yours when it isn't.

227
00:16:15,051 --> 00:16:16,891
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's already a form of constricting your mind.

228
00:16:17,592 --> 00:16:22,654
[SPEAKER_05]: And that constricted mind is not as... is more forgetful, basically.

229
00:16:23,554 --> 00:16:28,396
[SPEAKER_05]: Constricted mind will find harder to recollect things rightly or to broaden the context.

230
00:16:29,036 --> 00:16:31,737
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're focusing on your breath, you're forgetting the body.

231
00:16:32,985 --> 00:16:41,591
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, well, it's even worse, because you would probably be focusing on your breath for some reason, or the other, for some implication, and that's already a lack of context, due to the wrong view.

232
00:16:43,132 --> 00:16:44,813
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, sure, or sensations and so on.

233
00:16:45,653 --> 00:16:50,577
[SPEAKER_05]: But the point is, sati means memory.

234
00:16:50,597 --> 00:16:54,800
[SPEAKER_05]: So, right memory.

235
00:16:54,860 --> 00:16:59,803
[SPEAKER_05]: Right memory of the body enduring here, is a necessary basis for your experience as a whole.

236
00:17:00,742 --> 00:17:04,903
[SPEAKER_05]: Right memory of the properties of the body enduring here is a necessary basis for your experience as a whole.

237
00:17:06,284 --> 00:17:08,205
[SPEAKER_04]: The properties of the body being four elements?

238
00:17:08,785 --> 00:17:23,690
[SPEAKER_05]: No, properties of the... well, yeah, four elements, but properties of the four elements, meaning the universal characteristics, you did not create it, you have no say in its existence, it's unownable, it's headed to sort of decomposition.

239
00:17:23,930 --> 00:17:28,712
[SPEAKER_05]: All of those things are perpetually implicit in that body being there.

240
00:17:35,302 --> 00:17:35,662
[SPEAKER_05]: And same.

241
00:17:35,902 --> 00:17:36,683
[SPEAKER_05]: Ānāpānasati.

242
00:17:37,043 --> 00:17:37,984
[SPEAKER_05]: Memory of your breathing.

243
00:17:38,725 --> 00:17:41,787
[SPEAKER_05]: Memory of your acute, present, enduring action of breathing.

244
00:17:43,208 --> 00:17:48,212
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you're not accessing it through directly feeling your breathing and watching it.

245
00:17:48,652 --> 00:17:51,694
[SPEAKER_05]: You're accessing it through the image of your mind recollecting it.

246
00:17:53,035 --> 00:17:53,415
[SPEAKER_05]: Indirect.

247
00:17:57,438 --> 00:17:58,499
[SPEAKER_04]: As a situation...

248
00:17:58,519 --> 00:18:01,742
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you realize you could bring yourself through recollecting rightly,

249
00:18:02,768 --> 00:18:10,034
[SPEAKER_05]: Through the past, so to speak, through the memories, you could bring yourself to the present moment, here and now.

250
00:18:11,375 --> 00:18:14,277
[SPEAKER_05]: But see it through the memory of the present moment, here and now.

251
00:18:14,477 --> 00:18:16,719
[SPEAKER_05]: Not through you attending the present moment, here and now.

252
00:18:17,760 --> 00:18:18,520
[SPEAKER_05]: That's the difference.

253
00:18:19,201 --> 00:18:20,221
[SPEAKER_05]: That's the indirect way.

254
00:18:20,262 --> 00:18:21,542
[SPEAKER_05]: That's basically the yoniso.

255
00:18:22,163 --> 00:18:23,404
[SPEAKER_05]: That's how you rightly attend to it.

256
00:18:26,013 --> 00:18:34,237
[SPEAKER_02]: You could say that this is the most significant memory, that act of breathing, like in terms of your existence.

257
00:18:34,577 --> 00:18:39,099
[SPEAKER_05]: Oh, yeah, that can have all that... Those implications would then reveal themselves more and more.

258
00:18:39,840 --> 00:18:48,584
[SPEAKER_05]: But even if you, like, if you don't go, like, for the further steps of ānāpānasati, you know, breathing like this, and regard your body in this way, regard your mind in that way, just

259
00:18:49,257 --> 00:18:51,519
[SPEAKER_05]: Knowingly he breathes out, knowingly he breathes in.

260
00:18:52,079 --> 00:18:52,220
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

261
00:18:52,520 --> 00:18:54,241
[SPEAKER_05]: Because, again, memory, knowledge.

262
00:18:55,102 --> 00:18:56,423
[SPEAKER_05]: Memory is something that you know.

263
00:18:57,664 --> 00:19:00,647
[SPEAKER_05]: Things that you know, do you need to constantly observe them?

264
00:19:01,527 --> 00:19:01,687
[SPEAKER_05]: No.

265
00:19:02,228 --> 00:19:05,410
[SPEAKER_05]: Once you know them, once you understood them, they disappear.

266
00:19:06,912 --> 00:19:09,094
[SPEAKER_05]: And then they're just... the mind just recollects them.

267
00:19:11,275 --> 00:19:14,178
[SPEAKER_05]: Because mind and images, that is... that is its domain.

268
00:19:14,358 --> 00:19:15,499
[SPEAKER_05]: That is its feeding ground.

269
00:19:15,719 --> 00:19:16,600
[SPEAKER_05]: That's how it exists.

270
00:19:18,486 --> 00:19:37,663
[SPEAKER_05]: So, rather than trying to figure out the relationship between the two, which results in, as I said, appropriation of either or both, develop dispassion towards the entire domain by favoring certain images and memories rather than others.

271
00:19:38,658 --> 00:19:46,021
[SPEAKER_05]: So, favor those that are rooted in non-greed, non-aversion, that lead to dispassion, that lead to disenchantment.

272
00:19:46,041 --> 00:19:49,302
[SPEAKER_05]: So, favor the images of a subha.

273
00:19:50,063 --> 00:19:53,584
[SPEAKER_05]: Favor the subha aspects of the images that you recollect.

274
00:19:54,925 --> 00:20:00,067
[SPEAKER_05]: Favor the dukkha aspects of the images that you recollect.

275
00:20:02,968 --> 00:20:05,709
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, establish them peripherally as their basis.

276
00:20:08,257 --> 00:20:16,378
[SPEAKER_05]: And see, by the way, like now, remember something from like 20 years ago, some event, doesn't matter.

277
00:20:16,398 --> 00:20:24,120
[SPEAKER_05]: Obviously, you unabsorb yourself from here, to remember an image from 20 years ago, but that image from 20 years ago is here.

278
00:20:25,400 --> 00:20:36,582
[SPEAKER_05]: So, don't forget it to come back here, but while you remember it, see it being here, as it is this stove and the floor and all of us.

279
00:20:37,605 --> 00:20:39,226
[SPEAKER_05]: It is here, factually.

280
00:20:40,347 --> 00:20:40,968
[SPEAKER_05]: Here and now.

281
00:20:41,268 --> 00:20:50,015
[SPEAKER_05]: For you, from your point of view, that memory, doesn't matter how old, how far away it was, it exists here and now, in the same sense these other phenomena exist.

282
00:20:51,596 --> 00:20:53,958
[SPEAKER_05]: Because if it doesn't, what did you remember?

283
00:20:54,058 --> 00:20:54,918
[SPEAKER_05]: What are you talking about?

284
00:20:55,599 --> 00:20:58,561
[SPEAKER_05]: How did you even understand my question, my suggestion?

285
00:20:58,581 --> 00:20:58,942
[SPEAKER_05]: You wouldn't.

286
00:20:59,062 --> 00:20:59,742
[SPEAKER_05]: You wouldn't have.

287
00:21:00,743 --> 00:21:03,205
[SPEAKER_05]: The super is just the aspect that you can take upon anything.

288
00:21:04,177 --> 00:21:09,662
[SPEAKER_05]: The aspect of the ugly, which is unavoidable by the fact that... Well, that the body is disgusting.

289
00:21:09,902 --> 00:21:10,683
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

290
00:21:11,543 --> 00:21:14,066
[SPEAKER_05]: If the body were not disgusting, the asubha would not be possible.

291
00:21:14,786 --> 00:21:16,868
[SPEAKER_05]: But the body is factually disgusting.

292
00:21:16,888 --> 00:21:18,850
[SPEAKER_05]: Orifices, oozing, sweat, hair...

293
00:21:19,390 --> 00:21:24,354
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean, isolate those things outside of the intoxicated context of beautiful and they're quite disgusting.

294
00:21:24,815 --> 00:21:30,440
[SPEAKER_05]: As the suttas say, own mother would be disgusted with her child's body if she was to attend to it like that.

295
00:21:31,020 --> 00:21:31,881
[SPEAKER_04]: It's a disgusting thing.

296
00:21:33,458 --> 00:21:40,243
[SPEAKER_05]: It's just been buried under a kind of wrongly, directly attending to only the pleasing aspects.

297
00:21:40,503 --> 00:21:47,808
[SPEAKER_05]: So you develop this image now, that proliferated, and all this kind of ugliness is not even associated with it, while it's still made out of it.

298
00:21:50,590 --> 00:21:53,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you couldn't be having any perception of beautiful without the body.

299
00:21:53,852 --> 00:21:54,192
[SPEAKER_05]: Exactly.

300
00:21:54,333 --> 00:21:55,353
[SPEAKER_05]: And the body is factually ugly.

301
00:21:56,774 --> 00:21:58,175
[SPEAKER_05]: So you just have to uncover that.

302
00:21:59,076 --> 00:22:01,978
[SPEAKER_05]: But you won't uncover the ugliness if you directly try to see it.

303
00:22:03,523 --> 00:22:04,423
[SPEAKER_05]: But you will uncover it.

304
00:22:04,584 --> 00:22:15,189
[SPEAKER_05]: See, when I said, like in the early, old essays, when I said, like, oh, you find things there already given, or you find the body there already given, well, that means it.

305
00:22:15,269 --> 00:22:16,070
[SPEAKER_05]: You remember it.

306
00:22:17,771 --> 00:22:20,652
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, you find this body already given, existing in this situation.

307
00:22:20,672 --> 00:22:21,352
[SPEAKER_05]: How do you find it?

308
00:22:21,513 --> 00:22:24,774
[SPEAKER_05]: Oh, by remembering it as already being there.

309
00:22:26,195 --> 00:22:29,797
[SPEAKER_05]: So, finding things already existing independent of you, it's already a form of memory.

310
00:22:33,156 --> 00:22:36,180
[SPEAKER_05]: Just obviously, you have more immediate memories, and then you have more distant memories.

311
00:22:36,240 --> 00:22:37,942
[SPEAKER_05]: But either way, memory is real as such.

312
00:22:38,102 --> 00:22:38,703
[SPEAKER_05]: It exists here.

313
00:22:38,903 --> 00:22:52,140
[SPEAKER_05]: And, again, if you see that memory of the past event, or the present event, here and now, as something that's here and now, that is the most acute... Well, that's when mindfulness becomes sampajañña, when it becomes awareness.

314
00:22:57,304 --> 00:23:12,512
[SPEAKER_05]: But the point is to arrive through that acute awareness, here and now, not to directly leaping into it with your attention, as people who just think they should do it, but by fortifying that indirect way of seeing something that is here and now, without directly looking at it.

315
00:23:17,575 --> 00:23:24,138
[SPEAKER_05]: And you start that by seeing the body as a necessary basis that already endures there, and not forgetting it.

316
00:23:25,633 --> 00:23:28,354
[SPEAKER_05]: Always having a thread of recollection connected to it.

317
00:23:31,154 --> 00:23:40,836
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's why, obviously, as I said, container, sensory strength, containing yourself, containing bodily actions, are completely not negotiable.

318
00:23:41,616 --> 00:23:43,737
[SPEAKER_05]: They have to be developed beforehand, but then mentally as well.

319
00:23:43,757 --> 00:23:54,179
[SPEAKER_05]: You have to start containing your mind from a... just flowing into these abstractions, or fantasies, or delights, and so on, and just confine it to where it is.

320
00:23:55,810 --> 00:24:04,092
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, even if I allow this choice of fantasizing about this or that, it will be done so here and now on the base of this body.

321
00:24:04,192 --> 00:24:08,012
[SPEAKER_05]: So, let me not forget about the body.

322
00:24:10,793 --> 00:24:15,054
[SPEAKER_05]: And if you don't forget about the body entirely, to that extent, you'll be anchored rightly.

323
00:24:15,794 --> 00:24:20,635
[SPEAKER_05]: So, even thoughts that present themselves will not take you too far out where you, like,

324
00:24:22,509 --> 00:24:24,610
[SPEAKER_05]: Kind of lost the thread, lost the connection.

325
00:24:25,371 --> 00:24:28,732
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's the anchor of memory of the body, body memory.

326
00:24:34,055 --> 00:24:41,159
[SPEAKER_05]: And if you kind of start overthinking what is the memory, or somebody who listens to this might do that.

327
00:24:42,140 --> 00:24:44,581
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, remember anything.

328
00:24:45,882 --> 00:24:47,623
[SPEAKER_05]: Just remember anything from the past,

329
00:24:48,598 --> 00:24:52,740
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, okay, let me now, in the same manner, remember this body here.

330
00:24:53,981 --> 00:24:54,341
[SPEAKER_05]: That's all.

331
00:24:55,661 --> 00:25:02,285
[SPEAKER_05]: So, let me not directly attend to the body, let me just remember it that now, while I'm thinking this, oh, the body is here as a necessary basis.

332
00:25:02,985 --> 00:25:04,866
[SPEAKER_05]: And have that image endured, protect it.

333
00:25:05,966 --> 00:25:16,351
[SPEAKER_05]: Don't distract yourself from it, but obviously things will be coming and going, intensifying and fading away, regardless of what's coming or going away,

334
00:25:17,943 --> 00:25:21,885
[SPEAKER_05]: You try and sustain the necessity of the body being there for either.

335
00:25:28,608 --> 00:25:36,973
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, obviously, you can apply the same feelings in the other satipaṭṭhānas.

336
00:25:37,033 --> 00:25:45,157
[SPEAKER_05]: The mental satipaṭṭhāna, can you have anything, absolutely anything discernible, anything that you can perceive, that does not imply an image?

337
00:25:46,277 --> 00:25:46,797
[SPEAKER_05]: Mental image.

338
00:25:47,178 --> 00:25:47,938
[SPEAKER_05]: Inconceivable.

339
00:25:48,898 --> 00:25:50,299
[SPEAKER_05]: If you say, I see nothing.

340
00:25:50,499 --> 00:25:51,240
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just emptiness.

341
00:25:51,300 --> 00:25:52,781
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that's an image to that extent.

342
00:25:53,741 --> 00:25:59,364
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's why, if you were to practice memory of the body rightly, you would be practicing the memory of the mental images rightly.

343
00:26:01,685 --> 00:26:03,066
[SPEAKER_04]: Memory of the dhammas correctly.

344
00:26:06,788 --> 00:26:07,909
[SPEAKER_05]: Because, yeah, where are the thoughts?

345
00:26:07,969 --> 00:26:08,609
[SPEAKER_05]: Are they in your head?

346
00:26:08,909 --> 00:26:09,470
[SPEAKER_05]: In your skull?

347
00:26:12,431 --> 00:26:13,412
[SPEAKER_05]: But it's kind of implicit.

348
00:26:13,492 --> 00:26:14,512
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, see, oh, it's not there.

349
00:26:14,892 --> 00:26:15,473
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's kind of...

350
00:26:16,385 --> 00:26:18,587
[SPEAKER_05]: But, is it?

351
00:26:18,727 --> 00:26:21,790
[SPEAKER_05]: What's the measure for the insideness of your thoughts?

352
00:26:23,011 --> 00:26:23,872
[SPEAKER_05]: No, it's just an assumption.

353
00:26:24,632 --> 00:26:29,717
[SPEAKER_05]: And to the extent that you assume that your thoughts are in your head, to that extent they are not here and now.

354
00:26:31,755 --> 00:26:32,815
[SPEAKER_05]: They're over there and now.

355
00:26:33,016 --> 00:26:33,276
[SPEAKER_05]: True.

356
00:26:34,096 --> 00:26:36,997
[SPEAKER_05]: And the reason why this is important, like, so, why are you calling memory?

357
00:26:37,017 --> 00:26:44,661
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, because it clearly shows the different direction from which you have to approach, that indirect way, as I keep saying, to see your body indirectly.

358
00:26:45,081 --> 00:26:49,243
[SPEAKER_05]: Because even when people hear that, they will still try to attend to it directly.

359
00:26:49,583 --> 00:26:54,845
[SPEAKER_05]: Just think there is some secret back door, as opposed to, okay, I don't look here, but where do I look?

360
00:26:54,865 --> 00:26:56,166
[SPEAKER_05]: I still try to, like, find it.

361
00:26:56,826 --> 00:26:57,006
[SPEAKER_05]: No.

362
00:26:57,226 --> 00:27:00,348
[SPEAKER_05]: It's on the level of memory of anything else.

363
00:27:01,806 --> 00:27:06,648
[SPEAKER_05]: So, if you learn that that's where you have to stop, well, you're closer to doing it rightly.

364
00:27:06,968 --> 00:27:10,829
[SPEAKER_05]: It's less likely you'll be perpetually overdoing it or overly attending it.

365
00:27:11,509 --> 00:27:13,130
[SPEAKER_05]: So, remember something from this morning.

366
00:27:14,150 --> 00:27:14,450
[SPEAKER_05]: Okay?

367
00:27:14,871 --> 00:27:19,212
[SPEAKER_05]: Now, in the same manner, remember something from right now that sort of pertains to your body.

368
00:27:19,452 --> 00:27:20,613
[SPEAKER_05]: Remember your body right now.

369
00:27:22,033 --> 00:27:22,693
[SPEAKER_05]: And have that...

370
00:27:22,713 --> 00:27:23,333
[SPEAKER_05]: Okay, I remember that.

371
00:27:23,554 --> 00:27:24,474
[SPEAKER_05]: Okay, leave it there.

372
00:27:25,394 --> 00:27:27,215
[SPEAKER_05]: And try to now protect that image without

373
00:27:28,834 --> 00:27:31,436
[SPEAKER_05]: I tend to feel my knees and sensations and so on.

374
00:27:32,117 --> 00:27:32,998
[SPEAKER_05]: Completely unnecessary.

375
00:27:33,478 --> 00:27:34,859
[SPEAKER_05]: And actually, it's detrimental.

376
00:27:35,279 --> 00:27:36,901
[SPEAKER_05]: It takes you away from the right level.

377
00:27:45,768 --> 00:27:47,009
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's that body there.

378
00:27:49,785 --> 00:27:56,750
